# The continuous part of CBLIB 2014 that JAOS reads — 29 instances, pinned.
#
# H. A. Friberg, "CBLIB 2014: a benchmark library for conic mixed-integer
# and continuous optimization", Mathematical Programming Computation 8
# (2016), 191-214. The files are the gzipped CBF of
# cblib.zib.de/download/cblib2014/cont; the sha256 pins them. The reference
# is the primal objective of the set's own stat.set-cblib2014.csv (the
# library's solution files, from MOSEK); rows and columns are the model
# JAOS reads, which holds a cone over expressions as new columns and rows.
# The set's twelve filterdesign instances are left out: 71 to 872 MB each.
# The smallest of them, 2013_firL1Linfeps, reads in 3.4 s and solves in
# 59 minutes and 2.75 GB (bench/measurements/02-296/).
#
# The library's reference is a solution and not always the optimum. On
# nql and qssp JAOS reaches points that cbfeval.py, which reads the CBF
# file on its own, finds feasible to 5e-12, with objectives 2.5e-6 to
# 9.1e-5 below the library's, and the checker's dual bound puts each within
# 2e-9 of its optimum (bench/measurements/02-254/). Those eight rows carry
# another reference. qssp30 and qssp60 carry the value of the DIMACS
# library of mixed semidefinite-quadratic-linear programs (source dimacs7,
# archive.dimacs.rutgers.edu/Challenges/Seventh/Instances/tablestat.html,
# computed there by a primal-dual interior point code), which agrees with
# JAOS to 2.4e-7 and not with CBLIB. The other six carry the value 02-254
# certified (source 02-254): DIMACS gives nql30 and nql60 to four digits
# only, and has no value for the rest.
#
#   make cblib
#
# name sha256 rows cols reference source objconst
